package requests

// TwoFactorCode is the six-digit code from an authenticator app.
//
// The length rule gives nothing away: how many digits a TOTP code has is
// printed in the otpauth URI the user already scanned. That is what makes this
// different from the sign-in form, where a rule on the password field would
// tell a guesser which candidates not to bother with.
type TwoFactorCode struct {
	Code string `form:"code" json:"code" validate:"required,min=6,max=8,number"`
}

// TwoFactorRecovery is one of the codes issued at enrolment.
//
// No length or shape rule. The stored value is a hash, so the only thing that
// can be said about a submitted code is whether it matches one — and a user
// reading a code off a printout may well include the dash, drop it, or type it
// in lower case. Rejecting any of those here would turn a working recovery code
// into a lost account.
// The json tag is not decoration: a JSON body binds by tag and the decoder
// rejects a field it does not recognise, so without it recovery_code arrives as
// an unreadable request rather than as a code.
type TwoFactorRecovery struct {
	RecoveryCode string `form:"recovery_code" json:"recovery_code" validate:"required,max=64"`
}
